# Acids, Bases, and Salts: The pH Story

> Three theories, one logarithmic scale, and endless everyday
> consequences — from antacid tablets to acid rain. Master pH and
> the chemistry of acids, bases, and salts falls into place.

## 1. Three Theories of Acids and Bases

| Theory (Year) | Acid is | Base is | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Arrhenius (1884) | H⁺ producer in water | OH⁻ producer in water | aqueous solutions only |
| Brønsted–Lowry (1923) | proton donor | proton acceptor | covers NH₃; conjugate pairs |
| Lewis (1923) | electron-pair acceptor | electron-pair donor | broadest — includes BF₃ |

@diagram tree id="acid-base-theories" layout="tree" aria="Three acid-base theories of increasing scope from Arrhenius to Lewis"
  "Acid-base theories" -> "Arrhenius: H+ and OH- in water"
  "Acid-base theories" -> "Bronsted-Lowry: proton transfer"
  "Acid-base theories" -> "Lewis: electron pairs"
@/diagram

[definition]
A **conjugate acid–base pair** differs by exactly one proton:
HCl/Cl⁻ and NH₄⁺/NH₃ are pairs. After a Brønsted transfer, the old
acid becomes the new base and the old base the new acid.
[/definition]

## 2. The pH Scale

@math label="eq:ph"
pH = -\log[H^+]
@/math

pH runs 0–14 at 25 °C: below 7 acidic, exactly 7 neutral, above 7
basic. Each unit means a tenfold jump in H⁺ concentration — lemon
juice (pH 2.2) carries a million times more H⁺ than milk of
magnesia (pH 10.5).

@chart bar title="Approximate pH of common substances"
labels:
Lemon
Vinegar
Tomato
Coffee
Water
Blood
Soap

data:
2.2
3.0
4.2
5.0
7.0
7.4
10.0
@/chart

## 3. Indicators

| Indicator | Acid colour | Base colour | Useful range |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Litmus | red | blue | ~4.5–8.3 |
| Methyl orange | red | yellow | 3.1–4.4 |
| Phenolphthalein | colourless | pink | 8.3–10.0 |
| Universal indicator | full colour spread | full colour spread | 1–14 |

## 4. Strong vs Weak

| Strong acids | Weak acids | Strong bases | Weak bases |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃ | CH₃COOH, H₂CO₃, H₃PO₄ | NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂ | NH₃, methylamine |

[info title="Strength is not concentration"]
Strength measures ionization: a dilute HCl solution is still
strong (every molecule ionizes); a bottle of concentrated acetic
acid is still weak (ionization stays partial).
[/info]

## 5. Neutralization and Salt Types

Acid + base → salt + water:

@math label="eq:neutralization"
\mathrm{HCl + NaOH \to NaCl + H_2O}
@/math

| Salt type | Formed from | Example | Solution pH |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Normal | strong acid + strong base | NaCl, KNO₃ | ≈ 7 |
| Acidic | strong acid + weak base | NH₄Cl | < 7 |
| Basic | weak acid + strong base | Na₂CO₃, CH₃COONa | > 7 |
| Double salt | two salts in one crystal | potash alum | acidic |
| Complex salt | carries a complex ion | K₄[Fe(CN)₆] | — |

## 6. Everyday and Industrial Chemicals

| Formula | Common name | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| H₂SO₄ | sulfuric acid | "king of chemicals" — fertilizers, car batteries |
| HNO₃ | nitric acid | 3:1 with HCl = aqua regia, which dissolves gold |
| CH₃COOH | acetic acid | vinegar is about 5% |
| NaOH | caustic soda | soap making, drain cleaners |
| CaO | quicklime | heating limestone; cement |
| Ca(OH)₂ | slaked lime | whitewash; corrects acidic soil |
| Mg(OH)₂ | milk of magnesia | antacid |

Everyday acids: **citric** (lemon), **lactic** (curd), **oxalic**
(spinach), **acetic** (vinegar), **formic** (ant sting), and
**ascorbic** — vitamin C.

## 7. pH in Real Life

- **Hyperacidity** — excess stomach HCl; antacids (milk of
  magnesia, sodium bicarbonate) neutralize the surplus.
- **Tooth decay** — mouth bacteria turn sugar into acids; below
  pH 5.5, enamel's calcium salts start dissolving.
- **Acid rain** (pH below 5.6) — SO₂ and NOₓ from burning fuels
  dissolve in rain; it corrodes marble (the Taj Mahal's "marble
  cancer") and acidifies lakes.

[example]
**Plaster of Paris:** heating gypsum drives off water —
CaSO₄·2H₂O → CaSO₄·½H₂O — leaving plaster of Paris. Adding water
reverses the change and the paste sets hard; doctors cast broken
bones with it.
[/example]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Brønsted acid? | proton donor |
| H-free Lewis acid? | BF₃ |
| Phenolphthalein in base? | pink |
| Acidic salt? | NH₄Cl |
| Aqua regia? | 3 HCl : 1 HNO₃ |
| Tooth decay threshold? | pH below 5.5 |
